        <h2>History of Durham</h2>

        <article class="durham---history">

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>2000 BC</h3>

                <p>The earliest known settlement of people in the Durham area according to archeological evidence. settlement in the area since roughly 2000 BC.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>995 AD</h3>

                <p>Monks from Lindisfarne travel to the area we know as Durham as a place to bury St Cuthbert. This will become the site of Durham Cathedral.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1006 AD</h3>

                <p>Durham's defensive position helped the settlement withstand attacks from Scottish forces during the early medieval period.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1072 AD</h3>

                <p>Willam the Conquerer, orders the construction of Durham Castle. The castle was built to protect the city from Scottish invaders and to strengthen is control of northern England..</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1075-1080 AD</h3>

                <p>In this period, the Prince Bishops rose to the height of their power. political and military powers, effectively ruling the region as "Prince Bishops" because of Durham's importance near the Scottish border</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1093 AD</h3>

                <p>Construction of the iconic Durham Cathedral begins. Its initial construction would take under 40 years to complete and while it has had several redevlopments over the years it retains most of its Normal arhitecture.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1346 AD</h3>

                <p>The Battle of Neville's Cross takes place. The remaining English forces defeated the Scots near Durham during the reign of King Edward III and the battle endedec with the capture of the Scottish King David II.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1832-1836 AD</h3>

                <p>Political reforms gradually removed the remaining powers of the Prince Bishops, bringing Durham more directly under national government control..</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1832 AD</h3>

                <p>Durham University was founded, becoming one of England's oldest universities and helping shape Durham into a modern university city.</p>
            
            </div>

            <div class="durham---history--item">

                <h3>1986 AD</h3>

                <p>Durham Cathedral and Durham Castle were jointly designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site because of their historical and architectural significance.</p>
            
            </div>            

        </article>

